  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Evidence for each step of the dysbiosis → periodontitis → inflammation → bone loss → osteoporosis cascade**\n\n- **Dysbiosis initiates periodontitis** – Human studies show that a shift toward pathobiont‑rich communities (e.g., *P. gingivalis*, *T. forsythia*) coincides with the development of periodontitis and alveolar bone loss【PMID:24646638】. Animal work confirms that a dysbiotic microbiota, rather than a single species, is sufficient to trigger periodontal bone loss【PMID:25574929】, and metatranscriptomic profiling of human sub‑gingival plaque identifies functional signatures of dysbiosis that predict clinical progression【PMID:25918553】.\n\n- **Periodontitis drives a local inflammatory response** – The same dysbiotic biofilm provokes a host immune reaction that is “double‑edged”: protective yet bone‑destructive【PMID:24646638】. In mice, bacterial challenge induces IL‑1, TNF‑α and RANKL expression, establishing a causal link between the microbial trigger and non‑resolving inflammation【PMID:25574929】. In vitro, *P. gingivalis* suppresses LXRα/β in macrophages, altering cytokine output and highlighting a mechanistic route from pathogen to inflammation【PMID:25946408】.\n\n- **Inflammation mediates alveolar bone loss** – Elevated inflammatory mediators (IL‑1, TNF, prostaglandins, RANKL) directly stimulate osteoclastogenesis and the resorption of the supporting alveolar bone【PMID:25574929】. Mouse models lacking LXRs exhibit greater *P. gingivalis*‑induced cytokine production and greater oral bone loss, demonstrating that the inflammatory signal, not the bacteria per se, drives the skeletal damage【PMID:25946408】. Human histopathology shows that dysbiotic"
  },
